WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:38.370
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:39.967
happy international women's day.

00:00:39.967 --> 00:00:42.650
And in honor of international women's day,

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.980
we have a special guest,
someone who I know quite well,

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.640
Khan Academy's Chief
People Officer Regina Ross.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.070
We're going to talk about
her journey and careers

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.997
and developing careers
as a woman, or as anyone.

00:00:54.997 --> 00:00:57.850
But before we jump into that conversation,

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.020
I'll give my standard
announcement, first of all,

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.590
a reminder that we are a not
for profit at Khan Academy.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.140
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:09.490
please think about going
to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:12.320
Donations of all size
make a huge difference.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.140
Also wanna give a special
shout out to many folks

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:19.620
who support Khan Academy,
especially when the pandemic hit.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.090
And I think we're finally
surfacing from it,

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:26.030
but allowed us to really
do things like homeroom

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.070
and provide support for almost three X

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.000
the users that we were
seeing when the pandemic hit

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.390
so special thanks to AT&amp;T General Motors

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.003
and Fastly for that.

00:01:34.003 --> 00:01:35.660
And all of our other supporters

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.980
who really keep Khan Academy
going as a not for profit.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.430
And last but not least,

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.860
a reminder that there's a podcast version

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.510
of this live stream available
wherever you get your podcast,

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:48.420
Homeroom With Sal, the podcast.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:49.350
And now with that,

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.250
I'm excited to introduce
you to Regina Ross,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.882
Khan Academy's Chief
People Officer, hey Regina.

00:01:54.882 --> 00:01:57.223
- Hi Sal, I'm glad to be here.

00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:01.100
- So before we jump into
questions, I'll remind everyone

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.889
whether you're watching
on a YouTube, Facebook,

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:05.510
or I believe we're actually

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.620
live streaming on LinkedIn
today, post your questions,

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.550
our team members are gonna
help surface it to me

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.950
so that I can get to Regina,
topic is about leadership,

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.310
about managing organizations,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.143
But Regina,

00:02:17.143 --> 00:02:20.522
I always like to start pretty
early in people's journey.

00:02:20.522 --> 00:02:22.380
If you could tell us a little bit about

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.560
what you were like as a
kid, where did you grow up?

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.450
Did you think you would be a
Chief People Officer one day?

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:28.711
Did you even know what that is?

00:02:28.711 --> 00:02:31.704
Tell us a little bit
about yourself as a child.

00:02:31.704 --> 00:02:33.263
- Very interesting question.

00:02:33.263 --> 00:02:38.092
So I loved and thrived
in the academic setting.

00:02:38.092 --> 00:02:42.562
I was a nerd, a smart kid, loved school.

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:45.990
My father was in the military,
so we did travel a bit.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:50.700
But then we landed in at
home in Montgomery, Alabama,

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.331
and that's where I went to
school, graduated there.

00:02:53.331 --> 00:02:55.650
I was part of the gifted program

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:59.556
throughout elementary school
and I loved math and science.

00:02:59.556 --> 00:03:03.410
And so this really led to
my journey into engineering.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.866
Did you know, you knew I
was an engineer, right?

00:03:05.866 --> 00:03:08.040
- That's one of the reasons why,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.400
one of the many reasons we
definitely connected on that.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.233
- Yeah.

00:03:13.233 --> 00:03:14.180
Yeah, I love to travel.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:16.193
And let me just tell you the story,

00:03:16.193 --> 00:03:19.560
so I was really adept at math and science

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.003
and I had a high school chemistry teacher

00:03:22.003 --> 00:03:23.406
who says, hey, Regina,

00:03:23.406 --> 00:03:26.440
you should attend this
minority introduction

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:28.810
to engineering program, MIGHT program.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.430
It was a two week introductory
program at Auburn University.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.210
And that really opened my eyes
to the world of engineering

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:37.653
because I really didn't
know what an engineer was.

00:03:37.653 --> 00:03:39.570
So I was able to who explore

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.190
different disciplines of engineering,

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:44.070
and I landed on industrial engineering

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:47.214
because I loved the intersection
of people and processes.

00:03:47.214 --> 00:03:49.775
So I attended Auburn
University in Alabama.

00:03:49.775 --> 00:03:52.738
Didn't want to go too far away from home,

00:03:52.738 --> 00:03:55.170
but just far enough away.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.470
Graduated Summa, cum laude

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.643
and started my engineering career.

00:03:59.643 --> 00:04:03.410
And Sal, I'll tell you, even
though I did engineering,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.506
continuous process improvement,
mostly in manufacturing,

00:04:06.506 --> 00:04:09.720
I did transition into
operations, leadership,

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:10.580
sales leadership,

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.405
you know, I was a regional
logistics manager.

00:04:13.405 --> 00:04:17.530
I have always had a passion
for helping people develop.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:20.416
And maybe it's because of the
way that my chemistry teacher

00:04:20.416 --> 00:04:23.540
took an interest in me, and
like, hey, you can do more.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.620
So that's been my passion
for employee engagement,

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:27.702
leadership development.

00:04:27.702 --> 00:04:30.580
And I was eventually
tapped on the shoulder.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.271
I was an operations senior
leader at a big box retailer.

00:04:34.271 --> 00:04:38.640
And they're like, hey, Regina,
we want you to step into HR.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.943
And so the cause of my
culture focus leadership,

00:04:41.943 --> 00:04:44.930
I took the role and I
fell in love with HR.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:47.210
I say that I crossed over to the dark side

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:48.280
and I haven't looked back.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.490
And the thing about HR that I love Sal

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:54.040
is it really enabled me to
focus on connecting talent

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:56.243
and culture to the business outcomes.

00:04:56.243 --> 00:05:00.240
One thing you didn't know is
when I was a junior in college

00:05:01.577 --> 00:05:03.510
pursuing my engineering degree,

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:04.659
I wanted to shift majors.

00:05:04.659 --> 00:05:06.545
Like I wanna do something to help people.

00:05:06.545 --> 00:05:10.085
I was thinking of being in
ministry or being a nun,

00:05:10.085 --> 00:05:11.320
something.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:12.630
And my mom's like, no,

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.248
you're gonna finish
your engineering degree.

00:05:14.248 --> 00:05:19.040
And so it was me stepping into
HR years later, like, okay,

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:20.990
I get it, I'm able to help people,

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:22.572
I'm able to drive my business outcomes.

00:05:22.572 --> 00:05:25.537
And just seeing that
connecting of my passion

00:05:25.537 --> 00:05:28.271
and my purpose, I love what I do.

00:05:28.271 --> 00:05:31.550
- There's a bunch of really
interesting threads there.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:32.700
And just to connect the
dots, 'cause obviously

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.070
there's a lot of young
people watching this,

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:37.620
probably young men and
women watching this,

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:38.520
probably trying to figure out,

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.200
okay, is this an interesting path for me.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.190
How do you navigate this?

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:42.550
I know in high school, college,

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.680
everyone has a lot of angst,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:45.250
so like, what am I gonna do with my life?

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.307
And it sounds like you found this passion

00:05:48.307 --> 00:05:52.040
for math and science fairly early on.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.880
I think it helped that you had
other people believe in you.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.325
I definitely resonate with
that part of your journey

00:05:57.325 --> 00:05:59.810
and then industrial engineering,

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:00.930
which I don't think a lot of people know,

00:06:00.930 --> 00:06:03.090
but to your point, it's kind of like,

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.780
how do you optimize a factory?

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:08.800
You click on a button on
Amazon, now how does that,

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:10.413
how do they figure out how to put

00:06:10.413 --> 00:06:12.220
the right thing in the right package

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:14.940
and get it to the right
place, process design.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.590
So it's pretty broad, a set of things.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.370
And then you went into what
you kind of called operations,

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.160
but that would be more of like,

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.794
it still is process for
people in a lot of ways.

00:06:24.794 --> 00:06:25.627
- It is.

00:06:25.627 --> 00:06:26.460
Mm-hmm.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.030
- And then you went
into HR, human resources

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.823
and I think this is something,
I think I was a naive

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:35.100
initially when I was coming
straight outta college,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.420
I'm like, okay, I'm gonna go
work for a software company,

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:38.780
and that means that a software company

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:40.231
does nothing but write software.

00:06:40.231 --> 00:06:42.086
What do all these other people do?

00:06:42.086 --> 00:06:45.658
And I've now realized when
you have an organization,

00:06:45.658 --> 00:06:50.590
human resources, the
people function, it's core,

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.750
'cause it's all about
people doing everything.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:53.583
So.
- Absolutely.

00:06:53.583 --> 00:06:56.160
- How do you think about people operations

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.580
or human resources

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:00.692
to someone who was naive
like me in my early 20s?

00:07:00.692 --> 00:07:02.627
- You know what, that's what it is.

00:07:02.627 --> 00:07:04.520
And I was so fortunate Sal,

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:06.880
when I started at a particular company,

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.950
I won't say the name, but I
started as an operations leader.

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:12.280
So this is when I'm leaving
my engineering career,

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.810
going into operations leadership

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:16.654
at a distribution organization.

00:07:16.654 --> 00:07:21.450
And as a senior leader, I
had to do some onboarding,

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:25.890
and one of the courses was
thinking like an HR manager,

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:26.723
like what?

00:07:26.723 --> 00:07:27.904
I'm not an HR person,

00:07:27.904 --> 00:07:31.090
but the way that they
put it together is that,

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:34.128
you're not just leading or
managing your business processes,

00:07:34.128 --> 00:07:36.530
you're leading and
managing a team of people

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:38.060
who make it happen.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:40.133
And I some great mentors who taught me,

00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:42.771
Regina, if you take care of the team,

00:07:42.771 --> 00:07:44.980
they will take care of the business

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:46.210
and take care of the client.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:47.228
And so this is why

00:07:47.228 --> 00:07:51.420
I think I had this culture
focused leadership mindset

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:53.710
and approach that, yeah,
I'm an operations leader,

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:56.250
I'm an engineering leader,
but at the end of the day,

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:59.210
it's taking care of the people
and Sal, when I stepped into

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.190
my first full-time HR leader role,

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:03.394
the dots really connected.

00:08:03.394 --> 00:08:06.973
And I saw that potential of
really focusing full-time

00:08:06.973 --> 00:08:09.490
on helping the culture

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:12.590
and the people deliver on
the business priorities.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:16.910
And I will tell you the Khan
Academy job posting that I saw,

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:18.610
it really resonated with me.

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:21.790
And so I didn't have a
background in nonprofit,

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:25.160
I didn't have a background
in the educational industry.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:26.520
So when I saw the job posting,

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.730
I'm like, I don't know
if I'm going to be a fit,

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:31.470
but there was something in
that that kept pulling at me.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.450
And I'm just gonna read this verbiage

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:34.907
that was from the job description,

00:08:34.907 --> 00:08:37.379
it said that this role

00:08:37.379 --> 00:08:40.514
was to help foster a
high performance culture

00:08:40.514 --> 00:08:44.710
and a great place to work
that enables employees

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:46.190
to deliver their best work

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.532
towards attaining Khan Academy's mission

00:08:48.532 --> 00:08:51.290
while fulfilling their
personal aspirations

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:52.833
and career ambitions.

00:08:52.833 --> 00:08:53.870
Drop the mic.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:55.548
That was it for me, I was sold.

00:08:55.548 --> 00:08:57.178
(chuckles)

00:08:57.178 --> 00:08:58.727
- No, that's funny.
- That's what I'm doing.

00:08:58.727 --> 00:09:01.706
That's why I'm so excited to be here.

00:09:01.706 --> 00:09:06.706
And it's not, so I love Khan
Academy. I love what we do.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:09.440
And my kids, I use Khan
Academy with my kids

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.440
because even though I'm an engineer,

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:12.840
they struggle with math and science.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:14.540
I'm like, go figure.

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:17.760
But I know of the benefit and
the beauty of this product

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:18.731
and what we do.

00:09:18.731 --> 00:09:21.360
And when I saw this, like the mission,

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:24.309
and then me in my role
in people operations,

00:09:24.309 --> 00:09:26.530
and actually this
statement that I just read,

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:29.884
this is now our people operations
team missions statement,

00:09:29.884 --> 00:09:33.470
about really fostering that
high performance culture,

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:36.610
a great place to work, that
really does enable employees

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:37.810
to deliver their best work.

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:39.890
And so I'm just excited to be here.

00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:42.200
I love what I do, I love Khan Academy.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:44.030
- No, I think there's a
little bit of lesson in there,

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:47.920
which is, one, your journey
already took a couple of turns.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.150
And even in that last stage,

00:09:49.150 --> 00:09:50.240
before you came to Khan Academy,

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:51.180
there was a little part of you said,

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:53.352
well, I've never worked
at a non-for-profit,

00:09:53.352 --> 00:09:54.185
I wasn't in education.
- Yeah.

00:09:54.185 --> 00:09:55.110
- But this thing really resonated me.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:58.600
So you were able to kind of
not feel imposter syndrome

00:09:58.600 --> 00:09:59.987
and say, I'm gonna try it.
- Mm-hmm.

00:09:59.987 --> 00:10:04.680
- And as we all know, we
were excited to hire you,

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:09.410
but that reminds me of, I
could have never guessed

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:10.400
that I would've been

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:12.040
in this role either.
- Mm-hmm.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.680
- I remember in business school,
business school I went to,

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:16.460
they didn't really fail anyone,

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.440
but if they did fail someone,

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:20.090
they give one twos and threes in a class.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:21.750
And the only class that I got a three in

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:24.420
was called social
entrepreneurship, because.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:26.525
- No way.
- I was so cynical about the

00:10:26.525 --> 00:10:28.980
not for profit sector.
- Wow.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:29.813
- Back then.

00:10:29.813 --> 00:10:33.474
And fate, destiny, whatever,
irony would have it,

00:10:33.474 --> 00:10:36.184
that this is, I plan on doing
this the rest of my life

00:10:36.184 --> 00:10:37.520
now.
- Mm-hmm.

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:39.755
- So I think everyone's life
takes these twists and turns.

00:10:39.755 --> 00:10:40.870
I am curious,

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:43.324
and I always like to get into
the head every now and then,

00:10:43.324 --> 00:10:46.020
or pretty commonly, I
meet people, teenagers,

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:47.594
or people early in their careers,

00:10:47.594 --> 00:10:49.320
and there's so much uncertainty

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:50.153
of what they wanna do

00:10:50.153 --> 00:10:51.080
with their life.
- Mm-hmm.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:52.410
If you could go back in time

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.260
or at least to send a message back in time

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:58.228
to 20 year old Regina
or 30 year old Regina,

00:10:58.228 --> 00:11:01.800
what would you tell them?

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:02.633
- Yes.

00:11:02.633 --> 00:11:03.466
And I'm glad you asked that question

00:11:03.466 --> 00:11:07.411
because I have spoken to like my kids,

00:11:07.411 --> 00:11:09.860
and other young people that I'm mentoring

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:10.930
and trying to help.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:15.510
So my tips, one would be to
try different experiences

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:17.860
and versus just sticking
with only one thing

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:19.660
and only doing what you know well.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:21.270
So I've been in engineering,

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:22.780
I've been in operations leadership,

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:25.900
I've been in transportation,
I've done sales leadership.

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:29.061
Like I don't like that, I don't
wanna do a sales leadership.

00:11:29.061 --> 00:11:32.320
But it helped me to
understand what I liked

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:33.160
and what I didn't like,

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:35.178
what I was good at, what I wasn't good at.

00:11:35.178 --> 00:11:37.151
The second thing I would say is build

00:11:37.151 --> 00:11:40.440
and leverage a really
strong support network.

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.550
I call it a personal board of directors.

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:45.284
So you have mentors, sponsors, advisors.

00:11:45.284 --> 00:11:47.730
My career, I wouldn't be where I am

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.670
were it not for like my chemistry teacher,

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:52.020
who I'm still in touch with.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:54.380
You know, people who taught
me strategic planning,

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:58.206
people who taught me the
unwritten rules of the road

00:11:58.206 --> 00:12:00.670
in every corporation that I've been at.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:03.008
And so leverage that support network.

00:12:03.008 --> 00:12:05.250
One thing specifically for women

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.970
is that we tend to
build the relationships,

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:11.270
but according to "How Women
Rise" by Sally Helgesen

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:12.560
is that we don't leverage those.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.200
We think it's not good for us
to reach out and ask for help.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:19.300
So we've gotta build our
network, leverage the network.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.470
I would say another tip

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:23.260
or lesson learned throughout my career

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:25.913
is excel at the right things.

00:12:25.913 --> 00:12:28.520
So I can't be perfect at everything.

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:32.750
I know that I'm good at project
management and some things,

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:36.230
I'm not so good at maybe email management.

00:12:36.230 --> 00:12:39.210
And so it's about excelling
at the right things,

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:40.950
focus on what matters most.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.250
So particularly when you
go into an organization,

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:44.513
you're building your career,

00:12:44.513 --> 00:12:47.130
find out what's really important.

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:51.070
I've seen other women and I
myself have fallen into the trap

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:53.020
of sometimes just being heads down

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:54.688
and just trying to get a lot of work done.

00:12:54.688 --> 00:12:57.280
And you burn the candle at both ends,

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:58.987
and you're trying to do a lot of work,

00:12:58.987 --> 00:13:01.900
but you're not focusing on
what really matters the most.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:05.950
Another tip is know your worth
and advocate for yourself.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:08.113
This is something specifically for women

00:13:08.113 --> 00:13:09.880
and there's a lot of studies,

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:10.947
but one in particular is

00:13:10.947 --> 00:13:13.850
"Women in the Workplace
by McKinsey and LeanIn

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:15.570
where there's just a gap, right?

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:17.550
And this is why we're celebrating

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:20.993
on international women's
day, #breakthebias.

00:13:20.993 --> 00:13:24.380
So know your worth, advocate for yourself.

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:28.500
Typically men will negotiate
when it comes to salary time

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:30.860
or for promotions and women, we don't.

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:33.520
And so it's just really
important to make sure that

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:35.480
you know what it is that you want,

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:36.730
that you advocate for yourself,

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:39.290
you prepare yourself for that journey

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:41.840
and for those greater things.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:45.250
And this one Sal is so
important, I think this last one

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:48.214
is really defining
success on your own terms

00:13:48.214 --> 00:13:52.130
versus just avoid the comparison trap

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:53.930
because my journey

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:56.270
may be very different than
someone else's journey,

00:13:56.270 --> 00:13:57.720
and that's okay.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.900
I have taken a lot of twist
and turns in my career.

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:02.350
And in my development,

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:04.920
I wasn't really focused
on chasing the title

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.820
or the promotion or making this move here,

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:10.650
it was about me and my experiences,

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:12.690
my total quality of life.

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:17.210
And so I'm thankful and
grateful for where I landed,

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:19.770
but I tell you, there are
times where I look to the left

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:20.770
and look to the right, like,

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:22.810
oh, I should be at this level by now,

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:24.380
or this person is doing that.

00:14:24.380 --> 00:14:26.290
And I just really have to,

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:29.031
hey, let me define success for myself.

00:14:29.031 --> 00:14:33.120
One thing I would say for
younger people is that they think

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.565
they've gotta have it
all mapped out early on.

00:14:35.565 --> 00:14:36.880
And you really don't.

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:38.040
My kids struggled with this,

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:39.670
they were stressed out

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:42.210
because maybe as a junior in high school,

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:43.500
they didn't know exactly

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:45.100
what colleges they were gonna go to

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:46.615
and what they wanted to major in,

00:14:46.615 --> 00:14:51.320
this is a tool that I found
maybe five or six years ago,

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:53.890
but it's a Japanese concept called Ikigai,

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:56.430
a Japanese term that
means reason for being,

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.306
and I wish I had this years ago,

00:14:58.306 --> 00:15:00.410
but what it does in this Ikigai,

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:03.980
it's like a Venn diagram
where you are looking to

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:07.388
find that intersection
between what you love doing,

00:15:07.388 --> 00:15:11.060
what the world needs,
what you're good it at,

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:12.770
and then what you can be paid for.

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:14.907
And so when you find that intersection,

00:15:14.907 --> 00:15:17.010
that's that sweet spot or that Ikigai

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:19.270
your reason for being where like,

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:21.110
okay, I'm doing what I love,

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:23.040
I'm doing what I can be paid for,

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.270
you know, because some people
want to do a certain career,

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:28.090
but then they can't make a living at it.

00:15:28.090 --> 00:15:31.574
And so if you can find that
sweet spot, you are golden.

00:15:31.574 --> 00:15:34.670
Even if you can't find
that sweet spot in one job,

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:36.460
and this will be the
last thing I'll say Sal,

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:38.070
and turn it back over to you,

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:41.110
one thing I learned
from one of my managers

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:42.690
when I was a young engineer,

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:45.900
was Regina, you may not find
everything that you need

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:47.789
and get everything you
need out of this job.

00:15:47.789 --> 00:15:50.940
So make sure that you have
other outlets for your talents,

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:52.810
for your interest, for your passions.

00:15:52.810 --> 00:15:55.190
And this is where you
get into volunteering

00:15:55.190 --> 00:15:59.730
or you do some things on the
side that fuel your passion

00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:01.410
so that you're not getting frustrated

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:04.750
because you expect to get
everything out of that one job

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:07.760
or out of that first job.
and then you realize that

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:09.650
you're not getting
everything that you want.

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:13.395
So those would be my lessons
to the younger Regina.

00:16:13.395 --> 00:16:16.900
I wish I would've known these
things years and years ago.

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:17.733
- I love it.

00:16:17.733 --> 00:16:20.360
And I mean, just to summarize it,

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:22.810
'cause I think you could write
a book about this Regina,

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:23.810
personal board of directors,
(chuckles)

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:26.090
but don't just have 'em, leverage 'em.

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:27.130
- Yes.

00:16:27.130 --> 00:16:28.290
- Advocate for yourself.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:29.620
I do have a follow-up question about that

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:31.050
'cause I think that's an interesting thing

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:33.330
for a lot of folks, men and
women, but to your point,

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:36.110
it might be even a bigger
tension for some women,

00:16:36.110 --> 00:16:37.693
but advocate for yourselves,

00:16:38.844 --> 00:16:41.636
do things sustainably so
you don't burn both ends.

00:16:41.636 --> 00:16:43.420
- Mm-hmm.
- Find your own swim lane.

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:45.320
That's when I strongly strongly believe,

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:46.570
and I wasn't really happy.
- Mm-hmm.

00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:48.710
- On my career side until I had my own,

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:50.870
now I have my own swim lane.

00:16:50.870 --> 00:16:53.340
I wasn't a very competitive
swim lane before

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:54.630
as a hedge fund analyst.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:56.472
And no matter how much you succeed there,

00:16:56.472 --> 00:17:00.760
when you define yourself by
comparison in that swim lane,

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:02.530
you're just never going to be never going

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:03.363
to be happy.
- That's right.

00:17:03.363 --> 00:17:04.870
- So I love that swim lane.

00:17:04.870 --> 00:17:06.160
And that last piece is,

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:08.460
I often give a version of
that to a lot of young people

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:09.293
is just, yeah,

00:17:09.293 --> 00:17:10.920
make sure you always have
room for your passions.

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:12.060
No one job.
- Mm-hmm.

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:13.360
- Is going to be able to

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:14.800
check all of the boxes.
- That's right.

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:16.470
And so you're kind of overloading it

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:18.150
and then making it unsustainable

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:19.480
if you project everything.
- That's right.

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:21.820
Or you project everything onto it.

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:23.020
Well, one question,

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:24.200
you know, this is something
that I've struggled with

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:25.845
in my own career.

00:17:25.845 --> 00:17:28.580
And you mentioned this
especially important

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.480
for a lot of young women is
to advocate for yourself.

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:33.200
And it's often hard when
you're in the moment to say,

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:35.390
how hard do I advocate for myself?

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:36.850
How hardball do I play?
- Mm-hmm.

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:37.683
Because.
- Yeah.

00:17:37.683 --> 00:17:39.885
- There is a point where
people play too hardball.

00:17:39.885 --> 00:17:40.718
(chuckles)
People say,

00:17:40.718 --> 00:17:41.551
I'm sorry.
- Mm-hmm.

00:17:41.551 --> 00:17:42.506
- You know, like,

00:17:42.506 --> 00:17:45.970
and I've often seen this
even at Khan Academy,

00:17:45.970 --> 00:17:47.434
like at Khan Academy as you know,

00:17:47.434 --> 00:17:49.560
we take great pains that we say,

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:51.014
look, if someone's going to

00:17:51.014 --> 00:17:53.405
be doing a job at a certain level,

00:17:53.405 --> 00:17:56.160
this is what we're going to pay them.

00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:57.200
And we have gotten.
- Mm-hmm.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:59.680
- In the situation where someone's told us

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:01.140
their comp expectation is X,

00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:02.815
and we're like, well, this job,

00:18:02.815 --> 00:18:04.010
we're gonna pay you more.
(chuckles)

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:04.920
- Mm-hmm.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:06.520
- So it's, we're not negotiating,

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:08.460
we're gonna try to pay everyone with fair

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:09.500
'cause even though

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:11.260
you could probably get
away in the short term

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:12.490
with paying someone less

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:14.590
if they thought that that's
what they were worth,

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:16.300
at some point, they're
gonna ask their friends,

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:17.380
they're gonna realize.
- Mm-hmm.

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:18.340
- That they were being

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:20.090
uncompensated.
- That's right.

00:18:20.090 --> 00:18:22.110
And even the people who
are gonna be paid more

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:24.030
for that same job are like,
wait, this isn't a fair place.

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:25.150
Maybe I'm also getting

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:26.337
taken advantage somehow.
- Mm-hmm.

00:18:26.337 --> 00:18:27.880
- So as an organization,

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:30.900
it's I think super makes sense to pay,

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:33.600
if people are doing the same
level, same performance,

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:38.550
same job, pay them the same
and be transparent about it.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:41.120
But sometimes we have had
situations where people are like,

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:42.610
well, I need negotiate harder.

00:18:42.610 --> 00:18:44.860
And we're trying to be very
transparent saying, no,

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:47.014
we literally give the same pay.

00:18:47.014 --> 00:18:49.690
So we don't wanna be
based on negotiations.

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:52.390
So what advice do you have
to someone where it's like,

00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:53.223
they wanna negotiate,

00:18:53.223 --> 00:18:55.060
they wanna make sure they're
not taking advantage of,

00:18:55.060 --> 00:18:56.340
which I completely agree.
- Mm-hmm.

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:58.439
- But also there could be situations where

00:18:58.439 --> 00:19:00.799
it might be less productive?

00:19:00.799 --> 00:19:04.070
- Yeah, I would say one, do your research.

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:07.680
So be well informed because
you may be asking for

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:10.687
a number that's just really unrealistic.

00:19:10.687 --> 00:19:13.193
So, do your research, be informed.

00:19:13.193 --> 00:19:15.670
And then know for yourself,

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:20.600
like, what is the non-negotiable,
what are you willing to?

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:24.120
And because that puts you
in the power of choice,

00:19:24.120 --> 00:19:28.270
so you're not like, oh,
they only gave me this

00:19:28.270 --> 00:19:29.510
and I had to accept this.

00:19:29.510 --> 00:19:31.543
And I coach a lot of young women,

00:19:31.543 --> 00:19:34.106
like, no, you're not
having to accept this.

00:19:34.106 --> 00:19:35.800
You need to make a choice,

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:38.410
are you choosing to
accept it or are you not?

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:42.850
And so, and maybe you want this
number in your bare minimum,

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:43.683
it's like, you know what?

00:19:43.683 --> 00:19:45.460
I think I could be satisfied here.

00:19:45.460 --> 00:19:50.460
Then you make that decision of
I'm gonna be satisfied here,

00:19:51.090 --> 00:19:52.300
or I'm gonna push for here,

00:19:52.300 --> 00:19:55.300
and if I don't get this level,
then I'm willing to walk.

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:57.665
But you gotta have that internal dialogue

00:19:57.665 --> 00:20:01.270
and be very clear about
what your limits are,

00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:02.920
what your non-negotiables are

00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:05.170
and stand in that power of choice

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:07.080
so you're not feeling
like they did this to me,

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:10.870
or they made me, like no,
that, no, you have a choice.

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:11.780
You always have a choice,

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:13.900
it's either you accept it or you don't.

00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:15.508
Right.

00:20:15.508 --> 00:20:17.178
- Yeah, no, it's really powerful.

00:20:17.178 --> 00:20:21.120
I think throughout life,
whether it's negotiating comp,

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:23.108
but just everything in life,

00:20:23.108 --> 00:20:27.201
I still find this tension
of how much do I advocate?

00:20:27.201 --> 00:20:30.690
- Mm-hmm.
- Versus how much do I accept?

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:33.520
Or it is hard tension,

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:36.260
but I think being informed
is the right thing,

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:38.803
ask as many questions,
- Absolutely.

00:20:38.803 --> 00:20:40.980
- Zero in and then know
what you really want,

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:41.813
know what you

00:20:41.813 --> 00:20:42.770
really wanna deserve.
- That's right.

00:20:42.770 --> 00:20:43.700
- Don't go into something.

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:45.680
if you're feeling kind of
bad about it on day one.

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:46.513
- Exactly.
- Like,

00:20:46.513 --> 00:20:48.340
I think I'm underselling myself.

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:49.173
That's not gonna.
- Mm-hmm.

00:20:49.173 --> 00:20:51.510
It's not.
- Set anyone up for success.

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:53.220
And I'll see on the organizational side.

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:55.700
- Mm-hmm.
- This is what we try to do.

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:56.820
And Regina,

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:58.450
you and I have a lot of
conversations about this,

00:20:58.450 --> 00:20:59.760
how do we make
- mm-hmm.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:01.225
- Comp more transparent

00:21:01.225 --> 00:21:02.710
so we can give people.
- That's right.

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:03.543
- The confidence.

00:21:03.543 --> 00:21:06.170
'Cause the worst thing you
want is to be an organization

00:21:06.170 --> 00:21:09.092
or an environment where
you're wondering all the time.

00:21:09.092 --> 00:21:09.925
- Mm-hmm.
- Of like.

00:21:09.925 --> 00:21:11.650
- Yeah.
- Well, was this right.

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:13.530
- Am I being taken advantage of?

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:14.363
That's right.

00:21:14.363 --> 00:21:15.196
Yeah.
- Exactly.

00:21:15.196 --> 00:21:16.810
- And you wanna look at the total picture

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:19.920
of not just your base salary,
but your overall rewards,

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:24.282
you know, your time off, your
benefits, the quality of life,

00:21:24.282 --> 00:21:25.995
the type of environment.

00:21:25.995 --> 00:21:28.050
I have been many environment Sal

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:31.000
where we describe it as
the golden handcuffs.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:32.815
Well, yeah, I'm getting a big salary,

00:21:32.815 --> 00:21:36.380
a lot of stock and all that,
but it's a toxic environment.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.770
And so you gotta look at success for you

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:42.030
in terms of like total
rewards, life experience,

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:43.420
quality of life, all of that,

00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:45.662
and make the right decision for you.

00:21:45.662 --> 00:21:47.430
- Oh, makes a ton of sense.

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:52.230
Well, we have some
questions going on here.

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:53.540
So I think this is a question

00:21:53.540 --> 00:21:54.780
that a lot of people are wondering,

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:57.240
this is from Stephanie on social media,

00:21:57.240 --> 00:21:59.789
with the pandemic having
gone on two years now,

00:21:59.789 --> 00:22:01.700
what advice would you give to people

00:22:01.700 --> 00:22:04.523
who are feeling burned out in
their job, career, et cetera?

00:22:04.523 --> 00:22:06.020
- Yeah.

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:09.467
I really like Sal, thank you,
Stephanie for that question,

00:22:09.467 --> 00:22:12.560
this whole Ikigai exercise,

00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:16.516
like really taking that
time to be introspective

00:22:16.516 --> 00:22:17.930
of what do I really want?

00:22:17.930 --> 00:22:19.100
What am I passionate about?

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:22.062
What do I feel my purpose is?

00:22:22.062 --> 00:22:25.220
Am I in a space where I can achieve that?

00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:28.529
And if not, then evaluate, do
I need to make some changes?

00:22:28.529 --> 00:22:31.810
Sometimes it may need to be a job shift,

00:22:31.810 --> 00:22:34.753
sometimes it may need to
be putting some boundaries

00:22:34.753 --> 00:22:36.556
in your personal life.

00:22:36.556 --> 00:22:39.260
But, and we just don't even know.

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:42.266
We think this year is gonna
be a great year and we see,

00:22:42.266 --> 00:22:45.420
still just more emotional trauma

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:47.260
that we're all having to deal with,

00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:51.207
like evaluate what you need
and then determine what steps

00:22:51.207 --> 00:22:55.710
actionably that you can
take to get what you need,

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:56.543
whether it's boundaries,

00:22:56.543 --> 00:22:57.850
whether it's getting additional help,

00:22:57.850 --> 00:22:59.400
whether it's doing something

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:02.310
that feeds your passion in volunteering,

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:04.980
because the answer may look
very different from you,

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:06.340
Stephanie than someone else.

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:09.196
- That's great advice.

00:23:09.196 --> 00:23:11.700
And Regina, these homerooms,

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:13.790
especially when I talk to
really interesting guests

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:15.678
like you go way faster than I ever.

00:23:15.678 --> 00:23:17.640
- I know.
- Expected.

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:19.250
But we're already running low on time,

00:23:19.250 --> 00:23:22.749
but maybe just, if you could
kind of take us out with,

00:23:22.749 --> 00:23:24.410
you heard Stephanie's question,

00:23:24.410 --> 00:23:26.910
I think at all stages of people's careers,

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:27.743
there's this like.
- Mm-hmm.

00:23:27.743 --> 00:23:28.770
- Am I doing the right thing?

00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:30.717
Yes, all jobs have certain things that

00:23:30.717 --> 00:23:32.720
you're not super pumped about,

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:34.328
but it has other things that are good.

00:23:34.328 --> 00:23:36.040
How have you navigated it?

00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:37.500
When do you know it's time to switch

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:39.090
versus time to just perceive.
- Hmm.

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:40.120
- What you're doing differently

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:42.440
or try to tweak what
you're doing within the job

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:43.440
or any other advice.
- Hmm.

00:23:43.440 --> 00:23:47.436
- That you have for young
professionals or women everywhere?

00:23:47.436 --> 00:23:48.880
- Yeah.

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:50.190
One, so couple of things,

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:53.330
one is have your own like
personal mission statement.

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:54.430
This is something that I learned

00:23:54.430 --> 00:23:56.318
from "7 Habits of Highly
Effective People",

00:23:56.318 --> 00:23:58.590
like have my own personal
mission statement

00:23:58.590 --> 00:24:01.830
and then continually evaluate, where am I?

00:24:01.830 --> 00:24:03.200
Am I attracting towards that?

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:04.400
Or am I not.

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:07.180
Is the job that I'm doing,
the role that I'm serving,

00:24:07.180 --> 00:24:08.403
the way that I'm living,

00:24:08.403 --> 00:24:11.090
is it in alignment with
that mission statement?

00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:13.478
Am I moving towards my
purpose or am I not?

00:24:13.478 --> 00:24:15.270
Sometimes we have detours.

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:19.194
And I just say, evaluate
the pluses and the deltas

00:24:19.194 --> 00:24:22.310
and in your current
situation, do the pluses,

00:24:22.310 --> 00:24:25.840
outweigh the deltas, all
right, or vice versa,

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.570
if the deltas or the
negatives outweigh the pluses,

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:30.240
then it may be time for a change.

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:34.570
And you are the only person
who can determine that.

00:24:34.570 --> 00:24:37.080
There's a great person, Dr. Kim says,

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:40.780
if you always do what you've always done,

00:24:40.780 --> 00:24:43.770
then you'll always get
what you've always got,

00:24:43.770 --> 00:24:48.430
and only you can determine
if that is enough.

00:24:48.430 --> 00:24:49.990
So this is what I would leave you with,

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:52.600
evaluate where you're going, is it enough?

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:54.367
And if it's, not make a change.

00:24:54.367 --> 00:24:55.330
- I love that.

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:56.900
If you could send me that quote.

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:58.020
It's obviously.
- I will.

00:24:58.020 --> 00:25:00.065
- Such a obviously true quote,

00:25:00.065 --> 00:25:02.903
but something about the
phrasing that is so,

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:05.640
it's very poignant.

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:09.383
Well, Regina, thank you so
much for taking the time out.

00:25:09.383 --> 00:25:12.420
I think as everyone can
tell, I feel blessed

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:13.430
and Khan Academy is blessed

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:15.280
to have you as our Chief People Officer.

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:16.760
And I look forward to going
on this journey together,

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:19.030
both helping support
the team at Khan Academy

00:25:19.030 --> 00:25:20.850
in our journey to hopefully support

00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:22.947
10s or 100s of millions of
people around the world,

00:25:22.947 --> 00:25:24.730
but also be able to do things like this

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:26.000
and share your wisdom with

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:27.430
so many other people on the planet.

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:28.414
- Thank you Sal.
- Thanks (indistinct).

00:25:28.414 --> 00:25:30.497
- I'm so glad to be here.

00:25:32.871 --> 00:25:35.110
- Well, thanks everyone for joining.

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:37.610
I know I haven't been doing
these homerooms too frequently,

00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:40.160
I'll try to do a few more frequently.

